/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - In a lap joint one plate overlaps the other.
   - For structures, rivets of a tie bar are often arranged in a lozenge (diamond) pattern: one rivet in the first row, then two, then three.
   - Take one pitch length of the joint as a repeating strip.
   - Here n is the number of rivets in one pitch length and σt, τ, σc are the permissible stresses.
   - η = min(Pt, Ps, Pc) / (p·t·σt)
   - η is the efficiency (a fraction); p·t·σt is the strength of an unpunched plate one pitch wide.
   - The best design makes Pt equal to the smaller of Ps and Pc, which fixes the pitch.
   - An adhesive joint carries load through a thin layer of epoxy, acrylic, polyurethane or cyanoacrylate.
   - τavg is the average shear stress in the adhesive in MPa; P the load in N; b the joint width and l the overlap length in mm.
   - The shear is not uniform.
*/
